<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gilead</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gilead is the hilly region east of the Jordan River, on both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabbok">Jabbok</a>, in present-day north-western Jordan. Israelite tribes settled there, and it was long contested between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> and Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiglath-pileser-iii">Tiglath-pileser III</a> annexed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> around 733 BCE. The biblical 'balm of Gilead' refers to a resin associated with the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gilead.jpg" alt="Gilead" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jim Greenhill, U.S. Army · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Landscape_of_Jordan.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
